TITLE: Local LLM-Based Cyber Incident Analysis in Air-Gapped Networks via Teacher–Student Knowledge Distillation and Agentic Orchestration
TEXT: Article Local LLM‑Based Cyber Incident Analysis in Air‑Gapped Networks via Teacher–Student Knowledge Distillation and Agentic Orchestration Sunghun Jang 1 , MyoungRak Lee 1 and Taeshik Shon 2,* 1 Department of Defense Digital Convergence, Ajou University, Suwon‑si 16499, Gyeonggi‑do, Republic of Korea; jshjnc@ajou.ac.kr (S.J.); myoungrak2025@ajou.ac.kr (M.L.) 2 Department of Cybersecurity, Ajou University, Suwon‑si 16499, Gyeonggi‑do, Republic of Korea * Correspondence: tsshon@ajou.ac.kr Abstract Recent cyber incidents have become increasingly sophisticated through Living‑off‑the‑ Land (LotL) techniques that exploit legitimate behavior and multi‑stage attacks. This re‑ quires advanced reasoning capabilities to discern the attack contexts within fragmented large‑scale logs. However, closed network environments with physical network separation (air‑gapped), such as national critical infrastructures, restrict the use of high‑performance cloud large language models (LLMs), thereby limiting the adoption of cutting‑edge ar‑ tificial intelligence (AI)‑based analysis technologies. To overcome these constraints, this study proposes a Local LLM‑based intrusion analysis framework that operates indepen‑ dently within closed networks. The proposed framework combines (i) an Offline Knowl‑ edge Distillation technique that transfers the analytical reasoning process of external high‑ performance models to the Local LLM after a security review, and (ii) an AI agent orches‑ tration structure that controls the analysis procedure step‑by‑step and suppresses hallu‑ cinations. Experiments and validation using a public dataset (Atomic Red Team) demon‑ strated that the proposed model achieved a consistently higher detection accuracy (88.4%) and MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge mapping perfor‑ mance (0.91 F1‑Score) than existing general‑purpose Local LLMs. Furthermore, the pro‑ posed model suppressed hallucination rates to 6.2% through an automated verification mechanism and significantly improved analysis efficiency by refining large‑scale logs to focus on core events. This study quantitatively demonstrated that AI‑based intrusion inci‑ dent analysis can be automated using a single graphics processing unit server under con‑ trolled evaluation conditions. The proposed framework provides a practical prototype for intelligent security monitoring in closed‑network environments. However, the operational performance must be validated in real‑world deployments. Academic Editors: Guang Lu and Farhad Nooralahzadeh Received: 21 May 2026 Revised: 25 June 2026 Accepted: 29 June 2026 Published: 6 July 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. Keywords: cyber incident analysis; closed network; local large language model; incident analysis; offline knowledge distillation; artificial intelligence agent 1. Introduction 1.1. Background and Motivation Recent cyber incidents have evolved beyond single‑vulnerability attacks or simple malware infections. They now employ complex tactics, such as Living‑off‑the‑Land (LotL) techniques, that exploit legitimate tools within systems or maintain covert and long‑term Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 2 of 25 persistence [1]. As seen in the recent cyber conflict between Iran and Israel [2], destruc‑ tive attacks targeting critical national infrastructures, such as power, water, and nuclear facilities, are being executed with such sophistication that they can neutralize even physi‑ cally segmented network environments. Consequently, post‑incident breach analysis has become a critical procedure at the national security level, rapidly reconstructing the causes and paths of attacks from massive logs to prevent further damage. However, incident analyses in actual security operational environments still exhibit high human resource consumption and dependence on analyst expertise. Analysts must manually correlate the massive volumes of logs, events, scripts, and network records generated by various secu‑ rity tools. When attack activities are dispersed across time and space, fragmented analysis alone makes it difficult to grasp the full context of scenarios and causal relationships. This not only increases the analysis time but is also a primary cause of result variability depend‑ ing on the analysts’ capabilities. To overcome these limitations, research utilizing large language models (LLMs) for security analysis is actively progressing [3]. LLMs present new possibilities for analysis automation by integrating and interpreting diverse security data at the natural language level, inferring potential relationships between actions, and narratively summarizing com‑ plex incident flows. However, most existing LLM‑based approaches assume a cloud en‑ vironment, entailing the external transmission of sensitive data and dependence on an external infrastructure. This limits their applicability to organizations where security is paramount because they fail to meet their operational constraints [3]. The broader vulnera‑ bilities of traditional cloud security models [4] and emerging security issues of generative artificial intelligence (AI) [5] further reinforce this concern. Organizations such as military, government, and national infrastructure entities operate systems within closed network en‑ vironments that are physically isolated from the external Internet [6]. In this environment, cloud‑based LLM services cannot be utilized and the export of internal data is strictly con‑ trolled, creating a structural gap between the latest intrusion incident analysis technology and its field applicability. Therefore, a system capable of independent operations using only internal closed network resources with high‑level analytical capabilities is required. 1.2. Research Objectives and Contributions This study proposes a Local LLM‑based incident analysis framework capable of high‑ level inference without data export to overcome the structural constraints of closed net‑ work environments. The proposed framework uses a Local LLM deployed on a graph‑ ics processing unit (GPU) server within a closed network as the core inference engine. This is combined with a procedure that safely transfers the analytical reasoning process and judgment knowledge from an external high‑performance model (Teacher) to a Lo‑ cal LLM (Student) through Offline Knowledge Distillation. Furthermore, by introduc‑ ing an AI agent‑based orchestration structure that controls the incident analysis process through the staged steps of ‘Normalization–Candidate Selection–Semantic Reasoning– Reporting/Verification’, the input scale constraints inherent to Local LLMs are mitigated and hallucinations are actively suppressed, ensuring consistency and reproducibility of analysis results. The main contributions of this study are as follows: • Practical Framework: A comprehensive framework specifically designed for closed network (air‑gapped) environments is presented, demonstrating that AI‑based intru‑ sion analysis can be achieved using a single GPU server without external API calls or cloud resources. • Offline Knowledge Distillation: A Teacher–Student‑based mechanism that effec‑ tively transplants high‑dimensional reasoning from a Teacher model to a Student https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 3 of 25 model is designed, enabling a 7–8 B scale compact model to achieve expert‑level anal‑ ysis intelligence. • AI Agent Orchestration: A structured orchestration layer is employed that operates through a four‑step process, which substantially improves efficiency by refining large‑ scale raw logs into core threat indicators and managing them within the context win‑ dow constraints of the model. • Quantitative Validation: Through comparative experiments on 500 test scenarios, this study quantitatively demonstrated that the proposed framework achieved consis‑ tently higher detection accuracy (88.4%) and MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge (ATT&CK) mapping performance (0.91 F1‑Score) than ex‑ isting general‑purpose Local LLMs. • Reliability and Efficiency: The framework suppressed the hallucination rate to 6.2% and maintained a low memory overhead of only 15.3 GB of video random access mem‑ ory (VRAM), ensuring stable and sustainable operation with an average power con‑ sumption of 150 W. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews the related research trends. Section 3 presents the proposed framework. Section 4 presents the perfor‑ mance validation results, including self‑defined reliability evaluation metrics. Section 5 presents an in‑depth validation based on threat scenarios. Finally, Section 6 concludes the paper. 2. Background 2.1. Automated Intrusion Incident Analysis Automated intrusion incident analysis has traditionally evolved by combining rule‑ based correlation and signature‑based detection, and recent research has increasingly in‑ tegrated deep learning frameworks to enhance the identification accuracy in complex in‑ frastructures [7,8]. This approach is effective for rapidly identifying known attack patterns and offers the advantage of automatically processing large‑scale security events. In actual security monitoring environments, security information and event management (SIEM)‑ centric detection rules play a core role in the initial identification phase, with specialized intrusion detection systems (IDS) evolving further to secure diverse environments, such as mobile and industrial control systems [9]. However, when attack activities are amalga‑ mated with normal behaviors or distributed across multiple systems and time zones, the rule‑based approach has structural limitations. In multistage attacks or low‑intensity long‑ term attacks, individual events may fall within normal categories; however, malicious in‑ tent often becomes apparent within the broader context of the entire flow. Rule‑based analysis operates primarily on events that satisfy predefined conditions, limiting its ability to comprehensively interpret the context surrounding an incident, the causal relationships between actions, and the step‑by‑step progression of the attack. Furthermore, outputs from existing automated analyses are often provided as alert lists or event sets, failing to sufficiently meet the fundamental requirements of incident analysis: reconstructing the cause, path, and techniques of the attack into a single scenario. Consequently, analysts must manually connect and interpret the results from automated tools, a process repeatedly noted to increase analysis time, create dependency on exper‑ tise, and lead to discrepancies in the results [10]. Therefore, to overcome the limitations of static automation, research on generative AI‑based automated analysis has been gaining increasing attention. This approach can semantically interpret the unstructured context of security data and reconstruct fragmented information into logical scenarios. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 4 of 25 2.2. LLM‑Based Security Analysis Recently, research on security analysis using LLMs has been actively conducted as an alternative to overcome the limitations of intrusion incident analysis automation [11]. LLMs can interpret diverse security data, such as logs, codes, and behavioral descriptions, in an integrated manner at the natural language level. They differentiate themselves from existing analysis techniques by generating explanations that consider the semantic con‑ text of attack behaviors and the relationships between actions. Accordingly, diverse ap‑ plied research has been conducted, including security log summarization, vulnerability description generation, automated code review, malware behavior summarization, and attack technique mapping based on MITRE ATT&CK [12]. The MITRE ATT&CK framework, which is utilized as a global standard for incident analysis, provides a knowledge base that systematizes attacker tactics and techniques. Whereas traditional rule‑based systems focus on predefined pattern matching, LLM‑based analysis can contextually infer and map these tactics and techniques from unstructured logs. This goes beyond simple event classification, offering new possibilities for automated analysis by enabling the narrative reconstruction of an incident’s cause and progression. The ability to organize the incident flow into an explainable format supports report gener‑ ation and analyst decision making. However, many LLM‑based security analysis studies have assumed cloud environment utilization and feature architectures involving external application programming interface (API) calls or real‑time model updates. This can ne‑ cessitate transmitting sensitive log and incident analysis data externally or creating a de‑ pendency on external infrastructure and service policies during analysis. Consequently, applying these research outcomes identically within closed network environments with restricted external network access has some limitations. Furthermore, owing to the high reliability demanded by security analysis tasks, controlling the inherent hallucinations in generative models is essential [13]. In incident analysis, this phenomenon can lead to fabri‑ cation, creation of nonexistent attack logs, or critical false positives by misclassifying attack techniques into incorrect categories. Local LLMs operable in closed network environments exhibit higher hallucination frequencies than do large models because of their lower con‑ textual retention capabilities. Therefore, to ensure the integrity of the analysis results, a grounding technique that compares model‑generated sentences with the original logs in real time and an agent‑based verification mechanism that structurally controls this process must be implemented [14]. 2.3. Limitations of LLM Utilization in Closed Network Environments Closed network environments are widely used in organizations requiring high secu‑ rity levels, such as the military, government, and national infrastructures, where connec‑ tions to the external Internet are fundamentally blocked [6]. In this environment, LLM utilization methods, such as cloud‑based LLM API calls, online training, and real‑time model updates are impossible, and the external transfer of internal logs and incident anal‑ ysis data is strictly restricted. Therefore, meaning‑based analysis techniques designed for cloud LLMs cannot be applied similarly. Instead, an analysis system capable of indepen‑ dent operations within the network without reliance on an external infrastructure is re‑ quired. Owing to these constraints, simply deploying the model internally is insufficient to effectively utilize the LLM‑based analysis in closed network environments. To bridge this gap, it is essential to internalize advanced reasoning frameworks that allow smaller models to perform as high‑performance LLMs. Specifically, the adoption of Chain‑of‑Thought (CoT) [15] to elicit structured logical deduction and React [16] to syner‑ gize reasoning with step‑by‑step action is required to ensure analysis intelligence within isolated infrastructures. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 5 of 25 First, an offline update procedure based on knowledge distillation is necessary. It must safely incorporate the latest external analytical knowledge while optimizing the model size to deliver high performance within the limited computing resources of a closed network, thereby transferring intelligence. Second, an agent‑based operational system is required to manage and verify the inference process of the model step‑by‑step to ensure sta‑ ble analysis quality. Third, because security analysis requires providing evidence of results and ensuring traceability, an audit system must be implemented to structurally suppress model hallucinations and verify the factual accuracy of the generated reports against the source data [17]. 2.4. Local LLM Utilization in Closed Network Environments Local LLMs are increasingly being studied as alternatives to cloud‑based LLM ser‑ vices. They can operate directly on an internal infrastructure without external network con‑ nections. Therefore, they are suitable for closed network environments and high‑security organizations. Local LLMs also reduce the need to transmit sensitive logs, codes, or inci‑ dent analysis data outside an organization. This characteristic is important for environ‑ ments in which data exports are restricted. Recent open‑source models have different strengths, depending on the analytical task. For example, the Llama family [17] supports general‑purpose instruction execution. The Mistral family [18] is efficient for long‑context processing. The QWEN family [19] is suitable for code generation and analyses. The DeepSeek‑R1 model [20] emphasizes reinforcement learning‑based reasoning. These mod‑ els can support various tasks, including natural language understanding, large‑scale log analysis, code‑based security analysis, and complex causal reasoning. Recent studies have provided evidence that even 7B–8B‑scale models can perform meaning‑based analysis and stepwise reasoning, expanding the scope of their application. Table 1 compares the structural characteristics of large LLMs and 7B–8B‑scale Local LLMs. Large LLMs offer superior reasoning performance and extensive context‑processing capa‑ bilities; however, their resource consumption and security policy constraints make their de‑ ployment impossible in closed network environments. Conversely, although Local LLMs enable independent operations on internal servers, their limited parameter scale may re‑ sult in performance gaps compared to large models when inferring complex causal re‑ lationships in multi‑stage attacks or performing security assessments requiring advanced expertise. Therefore, research on optimization techniques and knowledge transfer method‑ ologies must proceed in parallel to maintain the resource efficiency of Local LLMs within closed networks while securing analysis intelligence comparable to that of large models. Table 1. Structural comparison between large LLMs and local LLMs (7B–8B). Category Parameters GPU Requirements Memory Requirements Deployment Environment External Data Transfer Air‑gapped Operation Operating Cost Large LLMs 70B–400B+ 10–100 GPUs Hundreds of GB Cloud Required Impossible Very High Local LLMs (7B–8B) 7B–8B 1–2 GPUs 40–80 GB On‑premise Server Not Required Supported Relatively Low Table 2 compares representative prior approaches with the proposed framework in terms of air‑gapped knowledge incorporation and reliability assurance. However, existing research on Local LLMs has often focused on single tasks, such as summarization, classification, or specific analytical functions. These studies provided useful automation capabilities. However, they did not fully address the end‑to‑end re‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 6 of 25 quirements of incident analysis. Incident analysis requires multistage reasoning, context integration, and the verification of results. It also requires a structure that can operate re‑ liably in a closed network environment. Two requirements must be addressed: the secure incorporation of external knowledge and reliable analysis results. The reliability includes hallucination suppression, evidence presentation, and reproducibility. Table 2. Comparison of prior approaches with the proposed framework. Category Cloud‑LLM Security Single‑Task Local‑LLM Proposed Framework (This Study) Air‑Gapped Knowledge Incorporation Not supported; requires external API or data egress On‑premise, but no secure knowledge refresh Offline distillation with secure refresh Reliability Assurance Partial; no source‑anchored verification Single‑shot output; no orchestration‑level audit Source‑anchored verification with HITL To address these gaps, this study proposes a framework that combines Teacher– Student‑based offline knowledge distillation [21] with AI agent‑based orchestration [22]. The offline distillation process transfers the external analytical reasoning knowledge with‑ out external network access. The agent layer decomposes and controls the incident anal‑ ysis process through normalization, candidate selection, semantic inference, and report‑ ing/verification. This study also prototyped a standard closed‑network operation model for defense and critical national infrastructure environments. These environments are sub‑ ject to strict security regulations and physical network segmentations. Therefore, they must rely on on‑premise server resources rather than cloud resources. To reflect this constraint, an experimental environment was built using a single GPU workstation with physically blocked external communications. This setting verified the practical feasibility of the pro‑ posed framework in high‑security operational environments. 2.5. Evaluation Metrics for Automated Intrusion Incident Analysis The output of automated intrusion incident analysis models is not a simple classifi‑ cation but a hybrid form combining descriptive explanations and structured mappings. Therefore, it is difficult to comprehensively verify the model performance using a single metric. Previous studies have generally combined three mutually complementary metrics: text similarity and quality assessment, verification of factual consistency in the generated results, and quantitative classification accuracy evaluation. First, text similarity and qual‑ ity evaluation metrics were used. These measures measure how similar the summaries or explanations generated by the model are to the ground truth data (reference sentences). Recall‑Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE) measures surface‑level tex‑ tual similarity based on n‑gram overlap with reference sentences and is widely used as a standard metric for automatic summarization evaluation [23]. Recently, to evaluate the contextual meaning similarity beyond simple word overlap, the BERTScore, which calcu‑ lates the semantic proximity between tokens based on sentence embeddings, was used alongside ROUGE to complement the limitations of surface‑level metrics [24]. Second, there are Factuality & Reliability metrics. Security analysis reports can have catastrophic consequences if they contain false information; therefore, it is essential to verify that the content generated by the model is factual and based on the input data (logs and events). Metrics such as Factual Consistency Checking (FactCC) are used specifically to detect hal‑ lucinations, a chronic problem in generative models [25]. This quantitatively controls the phenomenon in which models invent nonexistent facts or distort reality by either binarily classifying or probabilistically calculating whether the generated sentences logically con‑ tradict the source text. Third, quantitative classification and mapping accuracy metrics are https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 7 of 25 used. The incident analysis involves mapping the identified attack techniques to a stan‑ dardized framework (MITRE ATT&CK). In this multi‑label classification problem, the F1‑ score (Micro‑average), which is effective for data imbalance correction, serves as the core metric for evaluating how accurately the model extracts the correct tags. This study adopts the methodology of these previous studies but focuses on verifying the performance of the proposed framework in a multidimensional manner. This verification centers on the accu‑ racy of threat identification (Accuracy and F1‑score), which is essential from a practical perspective over simple sentence fluency, and the consistency of evidence and hallucina‑ tion occurrence rate, which form the foundation of trust in the analysis results. 3. Local LLM‑Based Incident Analysis Framework The proposed framework is designed to perform the entire incident analysis process independently within an air‑gapped network environment. As shown in Figure 1, the over‑ all architecture consists of three layers: (i) External Knowledge, (ii) Internal Learning & Model, and (iii) AI Agent Orchestration. These layers are modularized according to data flow, security boundaries, and functional roles, and they interact sequentially to support secure knowledge transfer, local model inference, and automated incident analysis. Figure 1. Overall architecture of the proposed air‑gapped incident analysis framework. 3.1. External Knowledge Layer This layer addresses the performance limitations caused by the parameter size con‑ straints of the closed network Local LLM. This is achieved by converting the analytical ex‑ pertise and reasoning processes of an external high‑performance model (Teacher Model) into data for transfer. • Threat Dataset: Multi‑dimensional raw logs were collected, including publicly avail‑ able threat intelligence (CTI), recent incident response (IR) reports, and Sysmon and Windows event logs, simulating attack payloads based on MITRE ATT&CK [26]. By securing time‑series data that captured attack tactics and procedures beyond simple single events, a high‑quality training source was built that enabled the model to un‑ derstand the context surrounding attacks. • Teacher Model: To establish a high‑fidelity knowledge source for distillation, a GPT‑4‑level LLM [27] was used, which was pre‑trained on extensive security do‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 8 of 25 main knowledge and recent vulnerability intelligence. In this framework, the Teacher Model is not merely utilized as a general‑purpose processor but is transformed into a specialized Expert Inference Engine through a structured prompt architecture. As shown in Figure 2, a specialized Expert Reasoning Internalization Prompt was de‑ veloped that enforced a Senior Security Analyst persona and multi‑dimensional cognitive constraints. This architecture mandates that the model interprets complex semantic corre‑ lations between fragmented log artifacts and defines their technical impact on the security posture of the system. Specifically, the prompt constrains the model to output a struc‑ tured four‑stage CoT reasoning, comprising Detection, Analysis, Hypothesis, and Action, alongside Subject‑Predicate‑Object (S‑P‑O) triplets. This ensures that the synthesized intel‑ ligence captures the underlying adversarial intent rather than superficial patterns, thereby providing essential ground‑truth data for the imitation learning process of the Student Model. To ensure the deterministic nature of knowledge synthesis and mitigate stochastic hallucinations, the Teacher model (GPT‑4) was configured with a Temperature of 0.1, Top‑ p of 0.9, and a Frequency Penalty of 0.1. These settings prioritize logical consistency over linguistic variety during the generation of the four‑stage CoT paths. Figure 2. Specification of the expert reasoning internalization prompt for teacher model distillation. • Knowledge Synthesis: Collected logs were input into the Teacher Model to synthe‑ size step‑by‑step CoT data based on the structured prompt architecture shown in Figure 2. This process was structured into four explicit cognitive stages. Stage 1 was Evidence Identification in which suspicious artifacts and anomalous indicators were detected. Stage 2 was the Threat Analysis, interpreting technical impacts and map‑ ping them to MITRE ATT&CK. Stage 3 was the Contextual Hypothesis, inferring the potential attack intent and predicting the next stages. Stage 4 was the Final Deter‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 9 of 25 Analysis Stage Stage 1: Evidence Identification Stage 2: Threat Hypothesis Stage 3: Technical Verification Stage 4: Final Determination mination, formulating a conclusive verdict and prioritizing actions. This structured synthesis guides the Student model to learn the logical reasoning paths of security experts via imitation learning, thereby structurally enhancing the inference capabili‑ ties of the compact model to discern complex attack contexts. Table 3 summarizes the structural framework of the distilled knowledge used for incident analysis. Table 3. Structural framework of distilled knowledge for incident analysis. Function in Knowledge Distillation Extracts specific indicators of compromise (IoC) or suspicious artifacts from raw logs. Formulates a potential attack scenario based on identified evidence. Cross‑references related logs to validate the hypothesis. Finalizes the mapping to a standardized framework. Practical Example in Training Dataset Identified suspicious use of certutil.exe with the URL cache flag in the command line. Hypothesis: The attacker is attempting Ingress Tool Transfer to download secondary malware. Verified outgoing network connection to an external IP immediately following the process execution. Conclusion: Mapping to MITRE ATT&CK T1105. Category Network Identity System Path • Data Sanitization: The synthesized knowledge assets underwent rigorous security reviews to anonymize or mask sensitive Personally Identifiable Information, such as real IP addresses and internal account names, by replacing them with virtual tokens to prevent data leakage. In Figure 1, this module is labeled as Data Sanitization as a high‑level architectural process, while the detailed sanitization and masking poli‑ cies applied within this process are summarized in Table 4. A standardized masking protocol was implemented to maintain analytical context while reducing the risk of data exposure. Table 4. Data sanitization and masking protocols. Item IP Address Username Hostname File Directory Sanitization & Masking Policy Replaced with INT_IP_[N] or EXT_IP_[N] Generalized to SEC_USER, ADMIN_USER, etc. Masked as ENDPOINT_[N] Generalized to C:\Users\MASKED_USER\… Rationale Preserve traffic directionality while masking topology. Protect PII while maintaining privilege context. Maintain multi‑stage lateral movement context. Protect user privacy during forensic analysis. Furthermore, data quality was standardized by normalizing unstructured text into structured learning schemas, such as JSONL, which accelerates model convergence and maximizes training efficiency. • Secure Transfer: The refined knowledge assets were migrated into the air‑gapped internal environment using a controlled, multi‑step transfer procedure rather than an unspecified protocol. First, the data underwent sanitization and malware scan‑ ning. Second, a SHA‑256 hash was generated for integrity verification. Third, the data were transferred using approved removable USB media. Fourth, the hash was ver‑ ified upon receipt inside the air‑gapped environment, continuing the Integrity Vali‑ dation step of Section 3.2. Fifth, the data were sandbox‑validated before entering the training pipeline. This procedure maintains the physical boundary through transfer https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 10 of 25 Stage Stage 1: Data Acquisition Stage 2: Model Adaptation Stage 3: Optimization on approved removable media across the air gap, and the logical boundary through sanitization, integrity verification, and sandbox validation. It is a procedural security control at the level implemented, not a vendor‑certified hardware product. 3.2. Internal Learning & Model Layer This layer establishes high‑dimensional inference knowledge that is imported through physical security media within an isolated internal infrastructure. It optimizes and builds a secure analytics engine that operates independently, without external network depen‑ dencies. • Integrity Validation & Loading: The integrity of the training dataset introduced into the closed network was verified by comparing its hash values to detect any tampering during transmission. After the final confirmation of data integrity within a sandbox in the secure zone, it was fed into the training pipeline. This is an essential security procedure to prevent model bias caused by contaminated data. • Local LLM Training: To ensure high‑performance incident analysis within a phys‑ ically isolated environment, an internal training process was conducted on inde‑ pendent GPU servers within the secure zone. Instead of employing computation‑ ally expensive full‑parameter updates, this framework utilizes a Low‑Rank Adapta‑ tion (LoRA) technique. This approach enables the 8B‑scale Student Model to effec‑ tively internalize the high‑dimensional reasoning logic of the Teacher model while maintaining a minimal VRAM footprint. As listed in Table 5, this internalization process operated through a systematic three‑stage pipeline. Stage 1 securely loaded and verified the integrity of the reasoning triplets. Stage 2 applied an architectural adaptation using low‑rank matrices to prevent catastrophic forgetting. Stage 3 op‑ timized the objective function to distill the teacher‑generated reasoning path for a standalone deployment. Table 5. Technical specifications of LoRA‑based local LLM training. Process Name Technical Specification & Rationale Reasoning Triplets ㆍ Integration of [Log + CoT + Label] with hash‑based integrity checks. LoRA Configuration Weight Internalization ㆍ Parameter‑efficient updates (r = 16, α = 32) on attention layers. ㆍ Cross‑entropy loss minimization of the reasoning path. • Student Model: The final Student Model inherits a four‑stage analytical framework (CoT) via fine‑tuning, enabling deep inference within an 8B‑parameter footprint. As shown in Figure 3, the model was optimized for standalone GPU environments us‑ ing FP16 precision to ensure real‑time processing of high‑load security logs within a 16 GB VRAM limit. This implementation leverages an internalized reasoning trigger, Detection, Analysis, Hypothesis, and Action, to activate a specialized security vocab‑ ulary, while maintaining a Zero‑drift policy through deterministic decoding. This in‑ tegrated approach ensures high‑throughput performance (sub‑50 ms) and absolute data sovereignty without external API dependencies. • Inference Engine Deployment and Practical Application: The Student Model serves as a core inference engine for autonomous AI agents. By implementing optimization https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 11 of 25 techniques, such as FP16 precision, the system ensures high resource efficiency, en‑ abling real‑time processing of high‑volume security logs, even on a single GPU server. Figure 3. Core implementation details of the standalone inference engine. 3.3. AI Agent Orchestration Layer This layer maximizes the analytical potential of the Student Model by systemati‑ cally controlling and automating the end‑to‑end incident analysis pipeline. An AI Agent functions as a central controller that decomposes high‑level security queries into exe‑ cutable tasks, thereby ensuring data integrity and reasoning precision through a four‑ stage process. • Data Normalization & Parsing: Raw heterogeneous log datasets collected from vari‑ ous security devices (e.g., endpoint detection and response (EDR) and Firewalls) were converted into a unified, structured schema. This stage eliminates system‑specific noise and redundant metadata, ensures data consistency, and optimizes the token efficiency for the subsequent deep inference stage. • Classification: To overcome the input constraints (Context Window) of the Local LLM while preventing the loss of stealth attack traces, the agent employs a Two‑Stage Hy‑ brid Filtering mechanism. • Stage 1 (Priority Scoring): Selects candidate events that exhibit high correlation with known attack indicators and high command‑line entropy. • Stage 2 (Anomaly‑based Sampling): Acts as a safety net by preserving atypical or long‑tail logs that may have low priority scores but exhibit high statistical deviation from the normal baseline of the system (e.g., rare process‑parent relationships or un‑ usual execution timing). These atypical events were classified using a z‑score thresh‑ old (|z| > 2.5) computed over the host‑level process parent frequencies and inter‑event timing distributions. To avoid pruning low‑and‑slow lateral movements, a host‑level sequence buffer was maintained across the observation window, and explicit reten‑ tion rules were applied to rare cross‑host authentication, remote execution, and un‑ usual parent‑child relationships, regardless of the Stage‑1 score. On the atomic test set, https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 12 of 25 Correlation Type Temporal (Time‑series) ㆍ ㆍ Spatial (Cross‑asset) ㆍ ㆍ ㆍ Behavioral (Causal Chain) ㆍ Objective (Final Goal) ㆍ ㆍ this hybrid filter preserved 99.4% of the high‑risk indicators, as reported in the Indi‑ cator Recall in Section 4.5.3. This figure reflects the preservation of atypical indicators rather than long‑window low‑and‑slow campaigns, in which the controlled dataset does not fully exercise, as discussed further in Section 6.3. This dual approach ensures that stealthy multistage attack components, which often masquerade as low‑priority background noise, are not pruned and are successfully delivered to the Student Model for contextual reasoning. • Deep Inference & Self‑Confidence Scoring: The agent leverages the distilled intelli‑ gence of the Student Model to conduct an in‑depth spatiotemporal correlation anal‑ ysis. At this stage, a Self‑Confidence Scoring mechanism was introduced to calculate the log probability of the generated tokens. If the cumulative confidence score (Sconf falls below a predefined threshold (e.g., 0.8), the agent flags the reasoning as Uncer‑ tain. This prevents potential hallucinations. It also triggers an immediate alert for human expert intervention (Human‑in‑the‑Loop; HITL). The fragmented clues were then reconstructed into comprehensive threat scenarios and mapped to the MITRE ATT&CK framework. Table 6 summarizes representative examples of deep inference logic and tactical mapping. Table 6. Representative case example of deep inference logic and tactical mapping. Fragmented Clues (Input Telemetry) Reconstructed Threat Scenario (Inference) w3wp.exe spawns cmd.exe 10 min later: net.exe used to scan network. The attacker exploited a web vulnerability to gain a shell and performed internal reconnaissance. ㆍ ㆍ Failed logins on Host A. Successful login on Host B from Host A via RDP. The attacker attempted brute‑force on Host A and successfully moved laterally to Host B. ㆍ ㆍ PowerShell with Base64 string. Outbound connection to unknown IP. An obfuscated script was executed to establish a ㆍ Command and Control (C2) ㆍ channel. Accessing ntds.dit file. Compressed file created in \Temp. The attacker is attempting to dump credentials for full domain takeover. ㆍ ㆍ MITRE ATT&CK Mapping (Output) T1190: Exploit Public‑Facing Application T1018: Remote System Discovery T1110: Brute Force T1021.001: Remote Desktop Protocol T1059.001: PowerShell T1132: Data Encoding T1003.003: NTDS Dumping T1560: Archive Collected Data • Reporting & Dual‑Verification (Traceability Matrix): In the final stage, the agent syn‑ thesized inference results into a structured security report. To move beyond simple suppression and achieve the near‑total elimination of hallucinations, the agent exe‑ cuted a Dual‑Verification protocol. This involved constructing a Traceability Matrix that programmatically linked every claim in the report to a specific, verifiable anchor in the original source logs (e.g., EventID, Timestamp and ProcessID). Any statement lacking 1:1 evidentiary mapping was automatically flagged for removal or manual audit. This ensured the absolute integrity and traceability required for high‑stakes security operations in closed‑critical infrastructure networks. 3.4. Closed Network Lifecycle Update and Secure Knowledge Refresh In a long‑lived air‑gapped deployment, fully automated online retraining is infeasible because the environment has no external connectivity. Therefore, the framework adapts to changing attack patterns through a periodic, low‑overhead refresh loop that minimizes manual effort rather than relying on full manual retraining. As analysts adjudicate Uncer‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 13 of 25 tain cases through the HITL console during normal operations, their confirmed corrections, together with newly imported threat intelligence on emerging techniques, are reused di‑ rectly as the training signal; therefore, no separate labeling campaign is required. These signals are converted into sanitized reasoning triplets, imported via approved media, and used to refresh only the LoRA adapter, configured with rank 16 and scaling factor 32 as listed in Table 5, after sandbox regression validation against the held‑out set confirms no degradation. The base model was never retrained, and no external connectivity was intro‑ duced at any stage. 4. Experimental Results and Analysis 4.1. Experimental Environment Experiments were conducted in a closed network environment that was physically iso‑ lated from external networks to validate the effectiveness of the proposed Teacher–Student‑ based domain‑specific knowledge distillation technique. • Hardware and Infrastructure Specifications: All experiments were conducted in a high‑performance server environment equipped with an NVIDIA A100 80 GB GPU. (NVIDIA Corporation, Santa Clara, CA, USA) External API calls and cloud resource utilization were completely excluded to satisfy the physical network segregation re‑ quirements under actual security regulations. This configuration demonstrates that a high‑level semantic analysis can be achieved even with a single GPU server. Although experiments were conducted on an A100‑80 GB server, peak memory consumption during inference remained below 15.3 GB, indicating that the proposed framework is deployable on commodity 24 GB GPUs. • Software and Inference Optimization: All models were executed in identical hard‑ ware and software environments (PyTorch v2.1.2 and Transformers v4.36.0). Consid‑ ering the limited computational resources characteristic of closed networks, inference was performed using FP16 (Half‑Precision) to achieve an efficient balance between inference speed and VRAM usage. Table 7 summarizes the hardware and software experimental environment used in this study. Table 7. Hardware and software experimental environment. Category Hardware CPU GPU RAM/Storage Software Inference OS/Language Framework Libraries Precision Specification Intel Xeon Gold 6226R (Intel Corporation, Santa Clara, CA, USA) NVIDIA A100‑PCIE‑80 GB (×1) (NVIDIA Corporation, Santa Clara, CA, USA) 256 GB DDR4/2 TB NVMe SSD (Samsung Electronics Co., Ltd., Suwon, Republic of Korea) Ubuntu v22.04 LTS/Python v3.10 PyTorch v2.1.2 + CUDA v11.8 Transformers v4.36.0, PEFT v0.7.0, Accelerate v0.25.0 FP16 (Half‑Precision) 4.2. Dataset Composition To rigorously validate the intrusion incident analysis capabilities of Local LLMs, this study constructed an analysis dataset based on the Atomic Red Team Project, which adopted the MITRE ATT&CK framework [26], a global threat intelligence standard. The Atomic Red Team defines the Tactics and Techniques (TTPs) of real attack groups (APTs) as standardized test cases, providing an environment optimized for evaluating behavior‑ based inference performance from unstructured security data. In this study, 3500 scenarios https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 14 of 25 Tier Single Single Multi Multi were constructed. Each scenario included system event logs (Sysmon and Windows Event Log) and network traffic records generated during execution in a virtual environment. 4.2.1. Scenario Reconstruction: Hierarchical Design Based on Analysis Depth Considering that simple command listings can distort the performance of analysis models, this study redefined individual Atomic Tests as complete behavioral scenarios where attack objectives, execution commands, and prerequisites are organically combined. To measure the analysis depth of the model multi‑dimensionally, the scenarios were re‑ structured into two tiers as follows: • Single‑stage Scenario: Evaluates micro‑level identification capability, which is the ability to accurately identify and technically interpret independent malicious actions, such as registry manipulation, permission authorization, and account creation. • Multi‑stage Scenario: Mimics the MITRE ATT&CK [26] kill chain model by causally linking two or more units belonging to different tactical phases. This study aims to validate the macro‑level semantic inference capability by reconstructing the full attack context and identifying correlations among temporally and spatially dispersed logs. Table 8 compares single‑stage and multi‑stage (Log A‑B‑C) evaluation scenarios. Table 8. Comparison of single‑stage vs. multi‑stage (Log A‑B‑C) evaluation scenarios. Scenario Type Credential Access Persistence Fragmented Clues (Log Inputs) Log A: procdump.exe ‑ma lsass.exe lsass.dmp Log A: net user/add attacker_account /password123 Full Intrusion Chain Log A: powershell.exe ‑enc... Log B: mstsc.exe/v:10.0.1.50 Log C: vssadmin.exe delete shadows Information Stealing Log A: reg.exe add HKCU\Software\... Log B: net view/all Log C: 7z.exe a data.zip C:\Confidential Target Inference Result (Chain of Thought) Identify: T1003.001 (LSASS Dumping). Analysis: Technical risk of memory dumping for password extraction. Identify: T1136.001 (Local Account). Analysis: Creation of a backdoor account for persistent access. Inference: 1. Initial C2 establishment (A) 2. Lateral jump to critical asset (B) 3. Ransomware preparation by inhibiting recovery (C). Conclusion: Macro‑level scenario reconstruction of an active Ransomware attack. Inference: 1. Establishing persistence (A) 2. Internal reconnaissance (B) 3. Staging for data exfiltration (C). Conclusion: Comprehensive mapping of an Information Stealing objective. These hierarchical scenarios served as foundational materials for the Teacher LLM [27] to generate high‑quality analytical reasoning processes during the offline knowledge dis‑ tillation process proposed in Section 3. In the experimental phase, they function as the ground truth to assess the accuracy of the model in deriving actual attack techniques from fragmented logs. 4.2.2. Dataset Partitioning and Learning Contamination Prevention Strategy To ensure model generalization performance and prevent data contamination caused by the exposure of training data to evaluation, scenarios were split based on their IDs. The 3500 scenarios were divided into training (2500), validation (500), and test (500) sets with an approximately 70:15:15 split with strict control over the purpose of each set, as outlined in Table 9. Specifically, the scenarios were split using a unique AtomicTest ID; therefore, https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 15 of 25 each ID appeared in exactly one split. To rule out leakage across different IDs, only volatile tokens (file paths, host names, UUIDs, and timestamps) were normalized and hash‑based duplicate checking was applied, confirming that no identical normalized command strings recurred across splits. While identical ATT&CK technique/sub‑technique labels may ap‑ pear in different splits, the underlying execution variant process contexts, environmental conditions, and non‑volatile argument structures remain distinct. Therefore, the test set measures generalization across unseen execution variants rather than unseen techniques. Table 9. Dataset composition and split statistics. Split Total Training Validation Test Scenarios 3500 2500 500 500 Ratio 100% 70% 15% 15% Usage Purpose ‑ Generating CoT knowledge via Teacher & Student model training Hyperparameter tuning & Early stopping decision Final performance evaluation (Completely held‑out from training) • Training dataset (2500 cases): Used as source data to transplant high‑quality CoT [15] reasoning processes and report‑writing knowledge generated by an external Teacher model (GPT‑4 [27]) into the Student model after undergoing a security re‑ finement process. • Validation dataset (500 cases): Used for monitoring during training to optimize the hyperparameters and determine early termination. • Test Dataset (500 cases): A dataset completely excluded from the knowledge dis‑ tillation and training processes. This serves as the final objective measure to verify whether the Local LLM can perform an accurate analysis based on internalized reason‑ ing mechanisms, rather than mere data memorization, when confronted with threat scenarios that it has not previously encountered. 4.3. Performance Comparison by Baseline Model This study selected four state‑of‑the‑art open‑source Local LLMs with parameter scales ranging from 7B to 8B as baselines. This selection aimed to control the performance bias due to the model parameter size and precisely analyze the pure effects of the model architecture and training strategies on the intrusion incident analysis performance. The detailed performances of the selected baseline models are listed in Table 10. This class was evaluated as possessing the optimal performance‑to‑resource efficiency achievable in a closed‑network environment using a single GPU. The selected models were chosen to represent the key capabilities for security analysis: general instruction execution, long‑text context processing, code interpretation, and stepwise reasoning. To ensure experimental fairness, all models were run in the same environment. Reflecting the unique characteristics of closed network environments, in which exter‑ nal infrastructure support is fundamentally blocked, the computational resource require‑ ments of each model were quantitatively reviewed. This demonstrated that the proposed model can operate stably even in a single GPU server environment (on‑premise) without cloud infrastructure dependency, and possesses practical resource efficiency to perform meaning‑based analysis requiring high computation. Beyond standard classification, be‑ havior under attack conditions was considered. Under obfuscation, such as command‑line encoding and renamed binaries, discussed in Section 5.1, general‑purpose baseline LLMs that lack internalized step‑by‑step reasoning tend to rely on surface lexical features and are https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 16 of 25 Model Llama‑3‑8B‑Instruct Mistral‑7B‑Instruct‑v0.3 Qwen2.5‑7B‑Instruct DeepSeek‑R1 Distilled Llama‑3‑8B (This Study) therefore more easily evaded, whereas the internalized CoT path reasons over behavior and intent and correctly mapped the evaluated single‑step obfuscated cases. As this com‑ parison is qualitative, a controlled head‑to‑head adversarial benchmark across all baselines was not conducted in this study and is listed as future work in Section 6.3. Table 10. Specifications and characteristics of baseline models. Parameters 8B 7B 7B 8B 8B Context Window 8K/128K 32K 32K/128K 8K~128K 8K~128K Key Characteristics General‑purpose instruction following & balanced performance Efficient long‑context processing & lightweight architecture Specialized in code analysis & mathematical reasoning Advanced reasoning capabilities based on Reinforcement Learning Security domain‑specific knowledge distillation & agent optimization 4.4. Definition of Evaluation Metrics This section defines the four key evaluation metrics used to objectively and re‑ producibly validate the performance of the proposed Local LLM‑based intrusion anal‑ ysis framework. As the analysis output is an analysis report format combining natu‑ ral language descriptions and structured mapping data, rather than simple classifica‑ tion values, the following metrics were calculated to measure the performance of the model comprehensively. • Overall Accuracy (Intrusion Detection Accuracy): Represents the percentage of times the final conclusion of the model (Malicious/Benign) for each scenario matches the correct label. Following conservative security monitoring principles, all cases in which a judgment is reserved (Uncertain) are considered undetected. • ATT&CK Micro‑F1 Score (Tactic Mapping Performance): Evaluates the match be‑ tween attack techniques identified by the model and the MITRE ATT&CK answer key. It applies a multi‑label classification perspective and adopts a Micro‑average method to address data imbalance. • Script Analysis Micro‑F1 Score (Core Malicious Behavior Identification Perfor‑ mance): This key artifact extraction metric measures whether the model accurately captures key malicious behavior clues within obfuscated scripts or commands. Its purpose is to quantify the sophistication of the analysis rather than the fluency of the generated sentences. • Hallucination Rate: The proportion of false information within generated reports that cannot be substantiated by the input data. It determines the presence or absence of evidence for each sentence using the FactCC methodology, forming the basis for traceability that allows analysts to immediately verify the authenticity of judgments. 4.5. Experimental Results Analysis and Resource Efficiency Evaluation 4.5.1. Comprehensive Performance Comparison Analysis The proposed framework and the four baseline models were evaluated using the same intrusion incident test dataset (500 types). The experimental results show that the proposed model achieved significant superiority over other models across all evaluation metrics, in‑ cluding intrusion detection accuracy, core malicious behavior identification, and halluci‑ nation rate. The detailed results are listed in Table 11. Of the 500 held‑out test scenarios, 31 cases (6.2%) fell below the confidence threshold (S_conf <0.8) and were flagged as Un‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 17 of 25 Model Llama‑3‑8B‑Instruct Mistral‑7B‑Instruct‑v0.3 Qwen2.5‑7B‑Instruct DeepSeek‑R1 Distilled Llama‑3‑8B (This Study) certain, triggering the HITL alert; per the conservative policy in Section 4.4 these were counted as undetected. Table 11. Performance comparison with local LLMs. Detection Accuracy 71.5% 69.8% 76.2% 79.5% 88.4% ATT&CK Mapping Score 0.68 0.65 0.74 0.78 0.91 Script Analysis Score 72.0% 68.5% 85.4% 83.1% 84.8% Hallucination Rate 28.4% 31.2% 19.8% 15.6% 6.2% • Overall Accuracy (Intrusion Detection Accuracy): General‑purpose models, such as Llama‑3‑8B (71.5%) and Mistral‑7B (69.8%), performed well in identifying individual events but showed limitations in discerning causal relationships among temporally and spatially dispersed logs. In contrast, the proposed model achieved 88.4% accu‑ racy, demonstrating that the CoT knowledge transferred via offline knowledge distil‑ lation structurally enhanced the inference logic of the compact model. • ATT&CK Micro‑F1 Score: Other models remained at F1‑Scores between 0.65 and 0.78 due to a lack of domain‑specific knowledge required when mapping unstruc‑ tured logs to the standard ATT&CK framework. The proposed model achieved 0.91, demonstrating that the security expert‑level reasoning approach inherited from the Teacher model operates effectively. • Script Analysis Micro‑F1 Score: Whereas Qwen2.5‑7B (85.4%), which specializes in code analysis, showed strength in script analysis, the proposed model (84.8%) achieved a comparable performance. This suggests the potential to attain specialized intelligence comparable to that of task‑specific models by applying knowledge distil‑ lation to general‑purpose models. • Hallucination Rate: All baseline Local LLMs exhibited prominent hallucinations, fab‑ ricating logs that did not exist in the analysis results or misclassifying attack tech‑ niques. The proposed framework reduced this rate to 6.2%, indicating that the fact‑ checking mechanism implemented in the report/verify phase of the AI agent success‑ fully compensates for the structural limitations of small models, rather than relying solely on model weight performance. Table 13 explains why and when these gains arise. When the framework is reduced to direct Student‑model inference without agent orchestration, accuracy decreases from 88.4% to 74.2% and the hallucination rate increases to 18.5%, indicating that the advantage stems from the structured analysis pipeline rather than raw model capacity, and is therefore most pronounced in multi‑ stage scenarios requiring causal correlation across temporally and spatially dispersed logs. Table 13 also reveals a clear division of labor: agent orchestration is the backbone, as removing it degrades every metric the most; the verification layer is a dedicated reliability control, as disabling it raises hallucination sharply while barely affecting accuracy or F1; and filtering plays a supporting role, preserving both detection accu‑ racy and mapping F1. The three components are therefore complementary rather than redundant, each addressing a different failure mode of compact Local LLMs. 4.5.2. Resource Efficiency and Closed Network Practicality Analysis Considering the resource constraints of physically isolated network environments, the GPU utilization, inference throughput, and power consumption of the proposed model were precisely measured. Experimental results demonstrated that the proposed frame‑ https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15132949 Electronics 2026, 15, 2949 18 of 25 Model Llama‑3‑8B‑Instruct Mistral‑7B‑Instruct‑v0.3 Qwen2.5‑7B‑Instruct DeepSeek‑R1 Distilled Llama‑3‑8B (This Study) work achieved optimized resource efficiency while maintaining high‑performance anal‑ ysis capabilities, thus proving its strong practical applicability. • Operational Suitability and Infrastructure Economics: Even under load conditions where AI agents and Student models operate simultaneously, it was confirmed that only up to 15.3 GB of VRAM was occupied. This indicates extremely low memory over‑ head owing to agent orchestration, enabling stable operation not only on enterprise‑ grade GPUs but also in standalone GPU environments. • Performance‑per‑Resource: While consuming computational resources comparable to state‑of‑the‑art inference‑specialized models, such as DeepSeek‑R1 (VRAM 15.3 GB, power 150 W), the proposed framework significantly outperformed them in detec‑ tion accuracy and reliability metrics. This demonstrates that analytical capabilities are maximized within limited computational resources through structural optimiza‑ tion, knowledge distillation and agent architecture, rather than simply increasing model parameters. • Power Consumption and Sustainable Operation: Average power consumption dur‑ ing system operation was measured to be approximately 150 W. This represents a significant reduction in operational costs and cooling demands compared with enterprise‑grade GPU‑based systems, which consume thousands of watts. Conse‑ quently, the proposed framework has proven to be the most practical alternative for delivering high‑performance analytical solutions while adhering to the strict resource guidelines of closed networks. Table 12 summarizes the computational resource usage and inference efficiency of the evaluated models. Table 12. Comparison of computing resources and inference efficiency. VRAM Usage (GB) 15.2 14.5 14.8 15.4 15.3 Throughput (tok/s) 52.4 55.1 54.8 48.2 51.8 Avg. Power (W) 148 142 145 155 150 Inference Time (s/case) 12.5 11.8 11.9 13.6 12.7 4.5.3. Ablation Study on Framework Components A series of ablation studies was conducted by systematically removing the core com‑ ponents to evaluate the individual contributions of each module within the proposed framework. Performance was measured across four configurations

