{"id":"R0363","study_type":"Local LLM-based intrusion analysis framework with comparative experiments and ablation studies","RQ1 task_stage":"Intrusion detection; incident scenario reconstruction, MITRE ATT&CK mapping, script analysis and report verification","RQ1 domain":"Air-gapped closed networks for military, government and national critical infrastructure environments","RQ1 orientation":"Defensive security monitoring and post-incident breach analysis","RQ2 architecture":"An AI Agent central controller orchestrates normalization, candidate selection, semantic reasoning and reporting/verification around a distilled Student model; host-level sequence buffer and source-log Traceability Matrix","RQ2 llm_used":"GPT-4 Teacher; Distilled Llama-3-8B Student","RQ2 adaptation":"Offline Teacher–Student CoT knowledge distillation using structured expert prompting; LoRA fine-tuning (r = 16, α = 32) and periodic adapter refresh","RQ3 autonomy_level":"Independently performs the end-to-end incident analysis pipeline within an air-gapped network; automatically filters logs, reconstructs threat scenarios, maps techniques and verifies reports","RQ3 hitl":"Confidence below 0.8 triggers human expert intervention; unsupported report claims are flagged for removal or manual audit, and analysts adjudicate Uncertain cases","RQ4 dataset":"3500 Atomic Red Team-based scenarios containing Sysmon, Windows Event Log and network traffic records; 2500 training, 500 validation and 500 test scenarios","RQ4 environment":"Virtual-environment attack execution; physically isolated single NVIDIA A100 80 GB server using FP16 inference","RQ4 metrics":"Detection accuracy, ATT&CK Micro-F1, script analysis Micro-F1 and hallucination rate; indicator recall, VRAM, throughput, average power and inference time","RQ4 baseline_type":"Four open-source general-purpose Local LLMs with 7B–8B parameters; framework component ablations","RQ4 headline_result":"88.4% detection accuracy, 0.91 ATT&CK Micro-F1 and 6.2% hallucination rate on 500 held-out scenarios","RQ4 compared_to":"Llama-3-8B-Instruct; Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; DeepSeek-R1 (8B)","RQ4 code_available":"not stated","RQ4 data_available":"not stated","RQ5 risks_addressed":"Sensitive-data leakage, contaminated training data and evaluation leakage; hallucinated evidence, incorrect technique mapping and pruning of stealth attack traces","RQ5 mitigations":"Data masking, malware scanning, SHA-256 integrity checks and sandbox validation; hybrid filtering, confidence-triggered HITL, source-anchored dual verification and split duplicate checking","author_limitations (verbatim)":"However, the operational performance must be validated in real-world deployments.","author_future_work (verbatim)":"As this comparison is qualitative, a controlled head-to-head adversarial benchmark across all baselines was not conducted in this study and is listed as future work in Section 6.3.","evidence (page/section)":"Study, tasks, setting and limitations: Abstract, §§1.1–1.2, 2.4; architecture, models, adaptation, autonomy, HITL and safeguards: §§3.1–3.4 (pp. 7–13); dataset and contamination controls: §4.2 (pp. 13–15); environment: §4.1, Table 7; baselines and future work: §4.3, Table 10 (pp. 15–16); metrics and results: §§4.4–4.5.3, Tables 11–12 (pp. 16–18)."}
END_JSON
